  <div class="article-body">

<p>In the political arena, Western politicians still frame China as a threat and strategic competitor. In the arena of daily life, Western young people are drinking hot water, practicing baduanjin, getting acupuncture, and binge-watching Chinese dramas — describing themselves with a shared label, "Chinamaxxing" (maxing out a Chinese-style life). This is both a split and a shift worth examining closely: China's overseas appeal is moving from "recognition for function" toward "identification with a way of life."</p>

<h2>One. Why it concentrated in 2026</h2>

<p>It is not the result of a single event, but of several factors reaching a tipping point in the first half of 2026.</p>

<p><strong>Institutional doors opening wider.</strong> National Immigration Administration figures show 82.035 million inbound foreign trips in 2025, up 26.4% year over year, with visa-free entries at 30.08 million, or 73.1% of the total. By the first half of 2026, the visa-free share rose further to 77.7%. Unilateral visa-free access now covers about 48 countries, 240-hour visa-free transit extends to 55, and Hainan's free-trade port grants visa-free entry to 86. The point is that this share keeps climbing rather than holding steady — the door really is opening wider.</p>

<p><strong>Products going out first.</strong> BYD sold 1.05 million vehicles overseas in 2025, up 145%, across 119 countries and regions; its full-year battery-electric sales of about 2.26 million passed Tesla for the first time. Pop Mart posted 2025 revenue of RMB 37.12 billion, up 184.7%, with overseas revenue of RMB 16.27 billion — 43.8% of the total — and the Americas up 748%. These are not abstract "Made in China" but a specific class of Chinese products — EVs, designer toys, consumer electronics — entering Western mainstream markets as "technologically leading and brand-trusted."</p>

<p><strong>Platforms amplifying.</strong> TikTok, a "globalized platform of Chinese origin," is the main stage; decentralization turns ordinary individuals into the carriers of the trend.</p>

<p><strong>Emotion underwriting it.</strong> A People's Daily Overseas Edition commentary put the core in one line: "finding a certain way of life in an uncertain world."</p>

<h2>Two. What it is actually "selling"</h2>

<p>Let's set one restraint first: reducing Chinamaxxing to "Western young people fleeing burnout culture" over-simplifies, and may distort. The intensity many Chinese see as "neijuan" (involution) is equally present in China; "Chinese-style life is more relaxed" is an open and contestable judgment.</p>

<p>A stabler way to break it down is three layers of emotional value (an analytical frame, not an academic conclusion):</p>

<ul>

<li><strong>Function layer:</strong> regular routines and self-care bring a sense of bodily control;</li>

<li><strong>Identity layer:</strong> wearing hanfu or mimicking a Chinese-style life is self-expression — "I belong to a certain circle";</li>

<li><strong>Meaning layer:</strong> choosing a Chinese-style life is a differentiated statement against the mainstream "always-on" life.</li>

</li></ul>

<p>Chinamaxxing's stickiness comes from hitting all three layers at once — a wellness technique, an identity label, and an emotional statement. One layer alone fades easily; three in resonance can settle into a way of life. But this remains an analyst's reading, not the participants' necessary motive.</p>

<h2>Three. What's different from 2008</h2>

<p>The 2008-Olympics-style output was state-led, centrally deployed, pushing outward, using official symbols (pandas, kung fu, Peking opera), with an audience of "spectacle and awe." The 2026 China trend is self-organized across platforms, individuals, and companies; the overseas world pulls inward and imitates spontaneously, using everyday lifestyles (hot water, baduanjin, dramas, consumption), with an emotional register of "identification, imitation, belonging."</p>

<p>In one line: 2008 was "state-narrative cultural output"; 2026 is "lifestyle cultural appeal." Because the latter is individually chosen, it is in principle more resilient to fluctuation — but that resilience is relative, because it leans heavily on institutional openness and has its own fragility.</p>

<h2>Four. The cold water that is due</h2>

<p><strong>First, policy dependence.</strong> Visa-free entries are 77.7% of inbound trips — nearly eight-tenths of the flow depends on institutional openness. The China trend is policy-driven supply, not purely spontaneous demand. If bilateral relations wobble and visas tighten, the interruption of offline experience will quickly transmit into cooler online sentiment.</p>

<p><strong>Second, the hard part is more fragile than the soft part.</strong> Culture can be "liked without taking sides"; AI and technology, tied to data and geopolitical security, fall under hard constraints. The soft, lifestyle part of the China trend withstands test better than the hard technology part. An honest observation: China's EVs and designer toys have hard data, but Chinese large models used daily by ordinary overseas consumers is, for now, more a developer and research-circle phenomenon — there is no multi-source evidence of ordinary Western young people using Chinese models in daily life.</p>

<p><strong>Third, how much of this trend is genuine demand?</strong> The financial data is hard fact — Pop Mart's overseas revenue and BYD's European sales are real money, costly to fake. But the other side deserves a look too: an independent think-tank investigation (chinaobservers.eu) alleges systematic recruitment of overseas influencers to shape a favorable narrative. The platform itself is of Chinese origin, and algorithmic amplification is unavoidable. The honest account is to admit: this trend is "real demand × official boost × platform amplification" stacked together; attributing it 100% to organic word-of-mouth, or 100% to propaganda, is equally inaccurate.</p>

<h2>Five. For companies and people, what's scarce is certainty</h2>

<p>Externally, this points to something: the more solid forms of national soft power live not in grand symbolic narratives but in ways of life people are willing to live every day, and in trusted products they choose again and again.</p>

<p>Internally, it is a reminder for anyone trying to hold their ground in the AI era: in a time of information overload and collapsing valuations, what is scarce is not information but a certainty that is "trustworthy, followable, and workable." What Pop Mart sells is less a toy than the emotion "between the certainty and uncertainty of opening a blind box"; what BYD sells is less a car than "the certainty that trusted technology brings."</p>

<p>And that certainty is the most essential foothold of human–AI collaboration.</p>

